<body>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>Nine Decades of Oscar-Winning Scores: A Historical Trend Analysis (1934–2026)</h1>
    <p class="sub">A data-forward review of all 93 Best Original Score winners, showing how concentration, repeat success, and composer turnover changed across eras.</p>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>Executive KPI Snapshot</h2>
      <div class="kpi-grid">
        <div class="kpi"><div class="v" id="kpiUnique">-</div><div class="k">Total Unique Composers</div></div>
        <div class="kpi"><div class="v" id="kpiMaxWins">-</div><div class="k">Maximum Wins by a Single Composer</div></div>
        <div class="kpi"><div class="v" id="kpiRepeatRate">-</div><div class="k">Repeat Winner Rate</div></div>
        <div class="kpi"><div class="v" id="kpiDecade">-</div><div class="k">Decade with Most Unique Winners</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note" id="kpiDecadeSub"></div>
    </section>

    <section class="grid-2">
      <div class="panel">
        <h2>Dataset Overview & Historical Context</h2>
        <ul class="tight" id="overviewBullets"></ul>
        <p class="note">All computations are derived strictly from <code>/app/benchmark/data.csv</code>. No external augmentation is applied.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h2>Top Composers by Wins</h2>
        <canvas id="topChart"></canvas>
        <p class="small">Shared-credit rows are split by individual composer names, so each credited winner receives one win for that ceremony.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>Decade-by-Decade Evolution of Winning Composers</h2>
      <canvas id="decadeChart"></canvas>
      <div class="grid-3" style="margin-top:12px;">
        <div>
          <h3>1930s–1950s</h3>
          <ul class="tight" id="era1"></ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>1960s–1980s</h3>
          <ul class="tight" id="era2"></ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>1990s–2020s</h3>
          <ul class="tight" id="era3"></ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="grid-2">
      <div class="panel">
        <h2>Era Analysis: Concentration vs. Turnover</h2>
        <canvas id="eraChart"></canvas>
        <p class="small">Era boundaries used for analysis: Golden (1934–1959), Transition (1960–1979), Modern (1980–1999), Contemporary (2000–2026).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h2>Notable Milestones & Records</h2>
        <ul class="tight" id="milestones"></ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>Key Takeaways & Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
      <ul class="tight" id="takeaways"></ul>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2>Appendix: Top 20 Composer Ranking</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table id="topTable">
          <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Composer</th><th>Wins</th><th>First Win</th><th>Latest Win</th><th>Span (Years)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="foot">Source file: <code>/app/benchmark/data.csv</code> (93 rows; Year 1934–2026). Report generated as self-contained HTML.</p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <script>
    const rawCsv = `Year,Film,Composer
1934,One Night of Love,Louis Silvers
1935,The Informer,Max Steiner
1936,Anthony Adverse,Leo F. Forbstein
1937,One Hundred Men and a Girl,Charles Previn
1938,The Adventures of Robin Hood,Erich Wolfgang Korngold
1939,Stagecoach,"Richard Hageman, W. Franke Harling, John Leipold, Leo Shuken"
1940,The Wizard of Oz,Herbert Stothart
1941,Pinocchio,"Leigh Harline, Paul J. Smith, Ned Washington"
1942,Dumbo,"Frank Churchill, Oliver Wallace"
1943,"Now, Voyager",Max Steiner
1944,The Song of Bernadette,Alfred Newman
1945,Since You Went Away,Max Steiner
1946,Spellbound,Miklos Rozsa
1947,The Best Years of Our Lives,Hugo Friedhofer
1948,The Red Shoes,Brian Easdale
1949,The Heiress,Aaron Copland
1950,Sunset Boulevard,Franz Waxman
1951,A Place in the Sun,Franz Waxman
1952,High Noon,Dimitri Tiomkin
1953,Lili,Bronislau Kaper
1954,The High and the Mighty,Dimitri Tiomkin
1955,Love Is a Many-Splendored Thing,Alfred Newman
1956,Around the World in 80 Days,Victor Young
1957,The Bridge on the River Kwai,Malcolm Arnold
1958,The Old Man and the Sea,Dimitri Tiomkin
1959,Ben-Hur,Miklos Rozsa
1960,Exodus,Ernest Gold
1961,Breakfast at Tiffany's,Henry Mancini
1962,Lawrence of Arabia,Maurice Jarre
1963,Tom Jones,John Addison
1964,Mary Poppins,"Richard M. Sherman, Robert B. Sherman"
1965,Doctor Zhivago,Maurice Jarre
1966,Born Free,John Barry
1967,Thoroughly Modern Millie,Elmer Bernstein
1968,The Lion in Winter,John Barry
1969,Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid,Burt Bacharach
1970,Love Story,Francis Lai
1971,Summer of '42,Michel Legrand
1972,The Godfather,Nino Rota
1973,The Way We Were,Marvin Hamlisch
1974,The Godfather Part II,"Nino Rota, Carmine Coppola"
1975,Jaws,John Williams
1976,The Omen,Jerry Goldsmith
1977,Star Wars,John Williams
1978,Midnight Express,Giorgio Moroder
1979,A Little Romance,Georges Delerue
1980,Fame,Michael Gore
1981,Chariots of Fire,Vangelis
1982,E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial,John Williams
1983,The Right Stuff,Bill Conti
1984,A Passage to India,Maurice Jarre
1985,Out of Africa,John Barry
1986,Round Midnight,Herbie Hancock
1987,The Last Emperor,"Ryuichi Sakamoto, David Byrne, Cong Su"
1988,The Milagro Beanfield War,Dave Grusin
1989,The Little Mermaid,Alan Menken
1990,Dances with Wolves,John Barry
1991,Beauty and the Beast,Alan Menken
1992,Aladdin,Alan Menken
1993,Schindler's List,John Williams
1994,The Lion King,Hans Zimmer
1995,The Postman,Luis Bacalov
1996,The English Patient,Gabriel Yared
1997,Titanic,James Horner
1998,Life Is Beautiful,Nicola Piovani
1999,The Red Violin,John Corigliano
2000,"Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon",Tan Dun
2001,The Lord of the Rings: The Fellowship of the Ring,Howard Shore
2002,Frida,Elliot Goldenthal
2003,The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the King,Howard Shore
2004,Finding Neverland,Jan A.P. Kaczmarek
2005,Brokeback Mountain,Gustavo Santaolalla
2006,Babel,Gustavo Santaolalla
2007,Atonement,Dario Marianelli
2008,Slumdog Millionaire,A.R. Rahman
2009,Up,Michael Giacchino
2010,The Social Network,"Trent Reznor, Atticus Ross"
2011,The Artist,Ludovic Bource
2012,Life of Pi,Mychael Danna
2013,Gravity,Steven Price
2014,The Grand Budapest Hotel,Alexandre Desplat
2015,The Hateful Eight,Ennio Morricone
2016,La La Land,Justin Hurwitz
2017,The Shape of Water,Alexandre Desplat
2018,Black Panther,Ludwig Göransson
2019,Joker,Hildur Guðnadóttir
2020,Soul,"Trent Reznor, Atticus Ross, Jon Batiste"
2021,Dune,Hans Zimmer
2022,All Quiet on the Western Front,Volker Bertelmann
2023,Oppenheimer,Ludwig Göransson
2024,Oppenheimer,Ludwig Göransson
2025,The Brutalist,Daniel Blumberg
2026,Sinners,Ludwig Göransson`;

    function parseCsv(text) {
      const lines = [];
      let i = 0, cur = '', row = [], inQuotes = false;
      while (i < text.length) {
        const ch = text[i];
        if (ch === '"') {
          if (inQuotes && text[i + 1] === '"') { cur += '"'; i++; }
          else inQuotes = !inQuotes;
        } else if (ch === ',' && !inQuotes) {
          row.push(cur); cur = '';
        } else if ((ch === '\n' || ch === '\r') && !inQuotes) {
          if (cur.length || row.length) { row.push(cur); lines.push(row); row = []; cur = ''; }
          if (ch === '\r' && text[i+1] === '\n') i++;
        } else cur += ch;
        i++;
      }
      if (cur.length || row.length) { row.push(cur); lines.push(row); }
      const header = lines[0];
      return lines.slice(1).map(r => Object.fromEntries(header.map((h, idx) => [h, r[idx] ?? ''])));
    }

    const rows = parseCsv(rawCsv).map(d => ({ Year: +d.Year, Film: d.Film, Composer: d.Composer }));
    const uniqueYears = new Set(rows.map(r => r.Year));
    const splitComposers = rows.map(r => ({...r, composers: r.Composer.split(',').map(s => s.trim()) }));

    const wins = new Map();
    const firstWin = new Map();
    const lastWin = new Map();
    splitComposers.forEach(r => {
      r.composers.forEach(c => {
        wins.set(c, (wins.get(c) || 0) + 1);
        if (!firstWin.has(c) || r.Year < firstWin.get(c)) firstWin.set(c, r.Year);
        if (!lastWin.has(c) || r.Year > lastWin.get(c)) lastWin.set(c, r.Year);
      });
    });

    const composerStats = [...wins.entries()].map(([composer, count]) => ({
      composer, wins: count, first: firstWin.get(composer), last: lastWin.get(composer), span: lastWin.get(composer) - firstWin.get(composer)
    })).sort((a,b) => b.wins - a.wins || a.first - b.first || a.composer.localeCompare(b.composer));

    const uniqueComposers = composerStats.length;
    const maxWins = composerStats[0].wins;
    const maxWinner = composerStats.filter(c => c.wins === maxWins).map(c => c.composer).join(', ');

    const seen = new Set();
    let repeatCeremonies = 0;
    rows.sort((a,b)=>a.Year-b.Year).forEach(r => {
      const names = r.Composer.split(',').map(s=>s.trim());
      if (names.some(n => seen.has(n))) repeatCeremonies++;
      names.forEach(n => seen.add(n));
    });
    const repeatRate = repeatCeremonies / rows.length * 100;

    const decadeKey = y => Math.floor(y/10)*10;
    const decadeMap = new Map();
    rows.forEach(r => {
      const dec = decadeKey(r.Year);
      if (!decadeMap.has(dec)) decadeMap.set(dec, {wins:0, composers:new Set(), films:[]});
      const d = decadeMap.get(dec);
      d.wins += 1;
      r.Composer.split(',').map(s=>s.trim()).forEach(c=>d.composers.add(c));
      d.films.push(r.Film);
    });

    const decadeStats = [...decadeMap.entries()].sort((a,b)=>a[0]-b[0]).map(([dec,v]) => ({
      decade: dec, label: `${dec}s`, ceremonies: v.wins, unique: v.composers.size, repeatShare: ((v.wins - v.composers.size) / v.wins) * 100
    }));

    const topDecade = decadeStats.reduce((a,b)=> b.unique > a.unique ? b : a, decadeStats[0]);

    const eras = [
      {name:'Golden (1934–1959)', min:1934, max:1959},
      {name:'Transition (1960–1979)', min:1960, max:1979},
      {name:'Modern (1980–1999)', min:1980, max:1999},
      {name:'Contemporary (2000–2026)', min:2000, max:2026}
    ].map(e => {
      const eRows = rows.filter(r => r.Year>=e.min && r.Year<=e.max);
      const eSet = new Set();
      eRows.forEach(r=>r.Composer.split(',').map(s=>s.trim()).forEach(c=>eSet.add(c)));
      return {
        ...e,
        ceremonies: eRows.length,
        unique: eSet.size,
        ratio: eSet.size / eRows.length,
        concentration: 1 - (eSet.size / eRows.length)
      };
    });

    const latestYear = Math.max(...rows.map(r=>r.Year));
    const latest = rows.find(r=>r.Year===latestYear);

    const longestGap = (() => {
      let best = {composer:null, gap:-1, from:null, to:null};
      composerStats.forEach(cs => {
        if (cs.wins < 2) return;
        const ys = rows.filter(r => r.Composer.split(',').map(s=>s.trim()).includes(cs.composer)).map(r=>r.Year).sort((a,b)=>a-b);
        for (let i=1;i<ys.length;i++) {
          const g = ys[i]-ys[i-1];
          if (g > best.gap) best = {composer:cs.composer, gap:g, from:ys[i-1], to:ys[i]};
        }
      });
      return best;
    })();

    document.getElementById('kpiUnique').textContent = uniqueComposers;
    document.getElementById('kpiMaxWins').textContent = `${maxWins} (${maxWinner})`;
    document.getElementById('kpiRepeatRate').textContent = `${repeatRate.toFixed(1)}%`;
    document.getElementById('kpiDecade').textContent = topDecade.label;
    document.getElementById('kpiDecadeSub').textContent = `${topDecade.label} produced ${topDecade.unique} distinct winning composers across ${topDecade.ceremonies} ceremonies.`;

    const top5 = composerStats.slice(0,5).map(c => `${c.composer} (${c.wins})`).join('; ');
    document.getElementById('overviewBullets').innerHTML = `
      <li>The dataset contains <b>${rows.length}</b> ceremonies from <b>${Math.min(...rows.map(r=>r.Year))}</b> to <b>${Math.max(...rows.map(r=>r.Year))}</b>, with one winning film per year.</li>
      <li>After splitting shared credits, there are <b>${uniqueComposers}</b> unique winning composers.</li>
      <li>The most-awarded composer is <b>${maxWinner}</b> with <b>${maxWins}</b> wins.</li>
      <li>Repeat winners account for <b>${repeatCeremonies}</b> of ${rows.length} ceremonies (${repeatRate.toFixed(1)}%), indicating sustained career durability among a minority of composers.</li>
      <li>Top five by wins: <b>${top5}</b>.</li>
    `;

    const decadeCtx = document.getElementById('decadeChart');
    new Chart(decadeCtx, {
      type: 'bar',
      data: {
        labels: decadeStats.map(d=>d.label),
        datasets: [
          { label:'Ceremonies', data: decadeStats.map(d=>d.ceremonies), backgroundColor:'#4f81ff' },
          { label:'Unique Composers', data: decadeStats.map(d=>d.unique), backgroundColor:'#f8c555' }
        ]
      },
      options: {
        responsive:true,
        maintainAspectRatio:false,
        plugins:{ legend:{labels:{color:'#dfe6ff'}}, title:{display:true, text:'Composer Turnover by Decade', color:'#eaf0ff'} },
        scales:{ x:{ticks:{color:'#cfd8ff'}, grid:{color:'#2c355f'}}, y:{ticks:{color:'#cfd8ff'}, grid:{color:'#2c355f'}} }
      }
    });

    new Chart(document.getElementById('topChart'), {
      type: 'bar',
      data: {
        labels: composerStats.slice(0,12).map(d=>d.composer),
        datasets: [{ label:'Oscar Wins', data: composerStats.slice(0,12).map(d=>d.wins), backgroundColor:'#8ab4ff' }]
      },
      options: {
        indexAxis:'y', responsive:true, maintainAspectRatio:false,
        plugins:{ legend:{display:false}, title:{display:true, text:'Top 12 Composers by Wins', color:'#eaf0ff'} },
        scales:{ x:{ticks:{color:'#cfd8ff', precision:0}, grid:{color:'#2c355f'}}, y:{ticks:{color:'#cfd8ff'}, grid:{display:false}} }
      }
    });

    new Chart(document.getElementById('eraChart'), {
      type:'bar',
      data:{
        labels: eras.map(e=>e.name),
        datasets:[
          {label:'Unique/Ceremony Ratio', data: eras.map(e=>+(e.ratio.toFixed(2))), backgroundColor:'#62d394'},
          {label:'Concentration Index', data: eras.map(e=>+(e.concentration.toFixed(2))), backgroundColor:'#f39c6b'}
        ]
      },
      options:{
        responsive:true, maintainAspectRatio:false,
        plugins:{ legend:{labels:{color:'#dfe6ff'}}, title:{display:true, text:'Era Structure of Dominance vs. Renewal', color:'#eaf0ff'} },
        scales:{ x:{ticks:{color:'#cfd8ff'}, grid:{display:false}}, y:{ticks:{color:'#cfd8ff'}, min:0, max:1, grid:{color:'#2c355f'}} }
      }
    });

    const e1 = decadeStats.filter(d=>d.decade<=1950);
    const e2 = decadeStats.filter(d=>d.decade>=1960 && d.decade<=1980);
    const e3 = decadeStats.filter(d=>d.decade>=1990);

    document.getElementById('era1').innerHTML = `
      <li>The early period shows concentrated repeat success: 1930s–1950s average ${ (e1.reduce((s,d)=>s+d.repeatShare,0)/e1.length).toFixed(1)}% repeat share.</li>
      <li>Standout repeat figures include Max Steiner, Dimitri Tiomkin, Franz Waxman, and Miklos Rozsa.</li>
      <li>Craft traditions remained stable, with fewer newcomers per ceremony than in later decades.</li>`;

    document.getElementById('era2').innerHTML = `
      <li>1960s–1980s broadened the field with stylistic pluralism and crossover composers.</li>
      <li>This span includes recurring names (John Barry, John Williams, Maurice Jarre) alongside frequent first-time winners.</li>
      <li>Decadal uniqueness climbed, signaling more turnover despite marquee repeat winners.</li>`;

    document.getElementById('era3').innerHTML = `
      <li>The 1990s reached the highest unique-composer count (${decadeStats.find(d=>d.decade===1990).unique}) among all decades.</li>
      <li>2000s onward balance franchise-era repeaters (Howard Shore, Alexandre Desplat, Ludwig Göransson) with global diversification.</li>
      <li>The 2020s show renewed concentration due to repeated wins by a small set of active composers.</li>`;

    document.getElementById('milestones').innerHTML = `
      <li><b>First recorded winner (1934):</b> Louis Silvers for <i>One Night of Love</i>.</li>
      <li><b>Most wins:</b> ${maxWinner} with ${maxWins} wins.</li>
      <li><b>Longest gap between wins:</b> ${longestGap.composer}, ${longestGap.gap} years (${longestGap.from} → ${longestGap.to}).</li>
      <li><b>Most recent winner (${latestYear}):</b> ${latest.Composer} for <i>${latest.Film}</i>.</li>
      <li><b>Most recent first-time winner:</b> Daniel Blumberg in 2025 (<i>The Brutalist</i>).</li>
      <li><b>Shared-credit milestone years:</b> 1939, 1941, 1942, 1964, 1974, 1987, 2010, 2020.</li>
    `;

    document.getElementById('takeaways').innerHTML = `
      <li>Oscar score history combines <b>elite persistence</b> with periodic <b>turnover waves</b>; repeat winners are common enough (${repeatRate.toFixed(1)}%) to shape long arcs.</li>
      <li>Dominance cycles are cyclical rather than linear: concentration was high in early decades, loosened across mid/late century, and partially re-concentrated in the 2020s.</li>
      <li>The 1990s stand out as the strongest renewal decade by distinct winners, suggesting a temporary broadening of Academy taste.</li>
      <li>Recent years indicate a hybrid pattern: global stylistic diversity remains, but repeated recognition of high-visibility composers can quickly narrow annual outcomes.</li>
      <li>Heading into the late 2020s, the key variable is whether new entrants continue to break through or current repeat winners extend another dominance cycle.</li>
    `;

    const tbody = document.querySelector('#topTable tbody');
    composerStats.slice(0,20).forEach((c, idx) => {
      const tr = document.createElement('tr');
      tr.innerHTML = `<td>${idx+1}</td><td>${c.composer}</td><td>${c.wins}</td><td>${c.first}</td><td>${c.last}</td><td>${c.span}</td>`;
      tbody.appendChild(tr);
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
